---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import LifeExpectancyCalculator from '@/components/tools/reference/LifeExpectancyCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="life-expectancy-calculator">
  <LifeExpectancyCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the life expectancy calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The life expectancy calculator reads the US period life table of the Social Security
      Administration. For each age and sex the table gives the probability of dying within a year
      (q), the number of survivors at each age out of 100,000 born (l), and the life expectancy (e).
      Your result is the life expectancy at your age; the chance to reach a later age is
      <code>l(later age) / l(your age)</code>.
    </p>
    <h2>Why life expectancy rises with age</h2>
    <p>
      Life expectancy at birth includes the deaths of infants, young adults and middle-aged people.
      Once you have lived past those ages, they no longer count against you, so your expected age at
      death goes up each year you live. The median age is the age by which half of the people your age
      have died.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits of a period life table</h2>
    <p>
      A period table uses the death rates of one year. If death rates keep falling, people live longer
      than the table says, so a cohort table for your birth year usually gives a few more years. The
      table is an average for the US population; health, smoking, income and many other factors change
      the risk for one person.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
